==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[ostler]]

===Etymology===
Syncopated form of {{term|hosteler}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|hostiler|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|hostelier|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|hostilarius|lang=la}}, from {{term|hospitalarius|lang=la}}, from {{term|hospitale|lang=la}} "inn", from {{term/t|la|hospitālis}} "hospitable", from {{term|hospes|lang=la}} "host, guest".  Both hostler and its alternate form "[[ostler]]" originally meant simply "innkeeper", and acquired a specific association with horses in the second half of the 14th century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈɒs.lə/|/ˈhɒs.lə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈhɑːs.lɚ/|/ˈɑːs.lɚ/}}
* {{audio|en-us-hostler.ogg|Audio (US)}}, {{audio|en-us-hostler-2.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person employed at an [[inn]], [[hostelry]], or [[stable]] to [[look after]] [[horse]]s; a [[groom]]
# {{context|by extension|lang=en}} A person employed to care for a [[locomotive]] or other large engine.

